You've missed the best part of the scam, credit cloning.

Walk into the shop, stack all the goods on one spot, with
at least one cursed item to keep your pet from wasting
time on fetching _goods_. On another spot (the one two
steps straight inside the door is _perfect_, so clear it
of possible cursed goodies first) drop all your cash,
however feeble a quantity (feel free to boost that
beginning total by selling stuff you brought with
you that the particular shop(keeper) will buy). Now
stand in or just outside the shop door, reward your
pet for fetching your cash, and each time it does,
march back in and drop that cash again for more
credit. This "clones" that credit, shopkeepers not
being very good bookkeepers about stuff, including
cash, that pets carry out the door.

When you have at least enough credit to pay for some
one thing in the previously built stack, pick that
thing up, pay for it, which will come out of your
credit, not your wallet, as long as your credit is
sufficient, immediately sell it back by dropping it
(getting some of the shopkeeper's hard-hoarded hard
cash instead of mere credit, at a substantial
discount dependent in part on your charisma), then
drop the cash on top of your existing cash on the
shop floor so your wallet stays empty and you don't
accidentally hand some gold directly back to the
shopkeeper to pay for something.

Why do you want more gold on the floor? So your pet
carries out more that you can carry back in and
drop for additional credit. Also, because that means
the gold _isn't_ in the shopkeeper's wallet any
more, so you have access to it without having to
brave the shopkeeper's armaments and famously bad
temper.

The higher your credit gets, the more things you can
pick up, pay for, and resell in a single cycle, the
bigger your pile of loot used to increase your
credit, so this process quickly accelerates.

You can keep this up, alternating with letting your
pet carry out the ever growing stash of hard cash
and you carrying it back in and dropping it for
more credit, until the shopkeeper runs out of gold
and starts weaseling with offers of "extra credit"
for what you offer for sale, instead of cash. At
that point immediately (q)uit the re-sale to the
shopkeeper, to avoid creating more stuff you must
buy back at the shopkeeper's high sales prices.

A few more iterations of the pet carries out, you
carry in and drop loop for the cash to build up
additional credit using your now substantial pile of
gold zorkmids, and you should be able to walk out
with all the goods, all the cash, and a huge, if
useless credit balance in the shop, leaving the
shopkeeper with that sandbagged feeling.

It seems funny that buying dear and selling cheap
leave you money ahead, but it is your pet's help
with confusing the shopkeeper's bookkeeping that
makes it all possible.

Lather rinse, repeat.  I managed to bankrupt one
dungeon shop above the mines entry, and all four
shops in the Mines Town with the most shops this way
in a single game (and _still_ got YASDd; Life's Not
Fair) for the first time recently, after lots and
lots of practice at arriving XP-less and being
careful not to suffer embarrassments in the shops.

It is really easy to bankrupt general stores, armor
shops, potion shops, jewelry stores, used literature
stores, and the like, because they carry lots of
pricy goods to buy and re-sell in a single cycle, so
a dozen or at worst two dozen tries will bankrupt
the shopkeeper. It is a task only for the really
obsessive to bankrupt delicatessens or the lighting
shop, which have fewer, cheaper goods that take
forever to bankrupt the owners (I went through well
over a hundred cycles to bankrupt Izchak without
committing mayhem instead, but that of course is a
matter of honor among NetHack players).

That's how I garnered enough cash to get a tourist
to a naked AC of -5 at a cross-aligned alter to
Offler, without a bit of mortality among shopkeepers
or altar-attending priests.  You, too, can
profoundly abuse NetHack features for fun and
profit, using my simple guide "Twoflower Sells
Retail Shop Bankruptcy Insurance", available on
better Ankh-Morpork bookdealer's shelves everywhere
for fewer zorkmids than you could even believe.

The moral of all this, to answer your original
question, is that with credit cloning, it really
doesn't much matter which character class you use,
you just have to wait a little longer if you don't
have a food treat to up the odds of your pet
fetching stuff, or a huge pile of cash with which to
begin the cycle, but the trick still works.

There are lots of inventory management details you
can invent for yourself to smooth this process. I
put all the limited quantity of lighting stuff in
a big box, then picked up and dropped that, just to
cut my keystrokes, for one example. I wasn't buying
or selling the box, just its contents, but I didn't
have to choose them to drop one by one, either, just
chose the inventory letter of the box to drop and
so sell it, and picked up the only item on the
location to acquire and then buy it.

